---
<DocPage eyebrow="UNITY / UI" title="Install and implement UI" lead="Add the Gua Unity package. The runtime starts automatically and reflects runtime UI Toolkit, uGUI, and TextMeshPro controls." description="Install the Gua Unity package and reflect runtime UI." section="unity">
  <h2>1. Add the UPM package</h2><Callout kind="tip" title="Unity package and test packages"><p>Download <code>com.link1345.gua-&lt;version&gt;.tgz</code> from the <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/releases/latest">latest Gua release assets</a> and install it through Package Manager's <strong>Add package from tarball</strong>. The UPM package contains native plugins for Windows x64, Linux x64, macOS x64/arm64, plus WebGL native libraries and <code>GuaWebMcp.jslib</code>. <code>Gua.Testing.Unity</code> is a separate package for the external test project. Match the test packages to the version shown in the downloaded file name.</p><p><code>&lt;version&gt;</code> means the actual version shown in Assets; for 1.1.1, the file name contains 1.1.1. For an existing test setup, choose the release matching that setup.</p></Callout><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Open the latest Gua release</strong><p>Download the Unity <code>.tgz</code> from Assets.</p></li><li><strong>Add it in Package Manager</strong><p>Choose <strong>＋ → Add package from tarball</strong> and select the downloaded archive.</p></li><li><strong>Import the sample</strong><p>Open <strong>Samples</strong> on the Gua package page and import <strong>Runtime UI Fixture</strong> for a scene containing the supported UI systems.</p></li></ol>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Player settings"><p>Use Windows x86_64, Linux x86_64, or macOS x64/arm64 with Mono and the .NET Standard 2.1 API Compatibility Level. Import TMP Essential Resources before building when TextMeshPro is present.</p></Callout>
  <h2>2. Understand automatic startup</h2><p>Godot game code calls <code>attach()</code> and <code>update()</code>. The Unity UPM package owns the equivalent work automatically, so each scene does not need a Gua initialization script.</p><div class="flow"><span>BeforeSceneLoad</span><b>→</b><span>Create runtime and driver</span><b>→</b><span>LateUpdate</span><b>→</b><span>Collect UI and dispatch actions</span></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Internal step</th><th>What it means for game code</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GuaUnityRuntime.EnsureStarted()</code></td><td>Creates the runtime and WebSocket bridge once before scenes load.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GuaUnityBootstrap.LateUpdate()</code></td><td>Collects UI after ordinary Update work for the frame.</td></tr><tr><td><code>BeginFrame → collect → EndFrame</code></td><td>Publishes UI Toolkit, uGUI, and TMP as one consistent semantic snapshot.</td></tr><tr><td>Action dispatch</td><td>Applies remote requests to Unity controls and returns request-correlated results.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><Callout kind="tip" title="What game code does not write"><p>The package manages <code>new GuaRuntime()</code>, <code>attach()</code>, per-frame <code>update()</code>, manual semantic registration, bridge startup, and disposal. Game code implements normal Unity UI plus stable IDs where needed.</p></Callout>
  <h2>3. Implement UI Toolkit controls</h2><p>Place standard controls below a runtime <code>UIDocument</code>; <code>name</code> or <code>viewDataKey</code> provides stable identity.</p><CodeBlock title="TitleScreen.uxml" lang="xml" code={`<ui:UXML xmlns:ui="UnityEngine.UIElements">
  <ui:Button name="start" text="Start Game" />
</ui:UXML>`} /><CodeNotes title="Read the UXML" items={[
    { code: '<ui:UXML xmlns:ui="UnityEngine.UIElements">', explanation: 'Creates a UXML document using standard UI Toolkit elements.' },
    { code: '<ui:Button name="start" text="Start Game" />', explanation: 'Creates the button; name=start is also a stable Gua ID candidate.' },
  ]} /><CodeBlock title="Short UI Toolkit example" lang="csharp" code={`using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.UIElements;

public sealed class TitleScreen : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private UIDocument document;

    private void OnEnable()
    {
        var root = document.rootVisualElement;
        var start = root.Q<Button>("start");
        start.clicked += () => Debug.Log("Start Game");
    }
}`} /><CodeNotes title="Read the UI Toolkit script" items={[
    { code: 'using UnityEngine.UIElements;', explanation: 'Imports UIDocument, VisualElement, and Button.' },
    { code: 'public sealed class TitleScreen : MonoBehaviour', explanation: 'This is an ordinary game MonoBehaviour, not a Gua base class.' },
    { code: '[SerializeField] private UIDocument document;', explanation: 'Receives the scene UIDocument through the Inspector.' },
    { code: 'private void OnEnable()', explanation: 'Registers the listener when the GameObject becomes active.' },
    { code: 'document.rootVisualElement', explanation: 'Gets the root of the VisualElement tree created from UXML.' },
    { code: 'root.Q<Button>("start")', explanation: 'Finds the UXML button by name; Gua uses that name for identity too.' },
    { code: 'start.clicked += ...', explanation: 'Registers a normal Unity listener. Remote Gua clicks follow the same event path.' },
  ]} /><p>Buttons, toggles, text fields, sliders, dropdowns, and lists are reflected automatically.</p>
  <h2>4. Implement uGUI or TextMeshPro controls</h2><p>Place standard controls below a Canvas. Add <code>GuaId</code> when a GameObject needs a stable long-lived test ID.</p><CodeBlock title="Short uGUI example" lang="csharp" code={`using Gua.Unity;
using UnityEngine;
using UnityEngine.UI;

public sealed class TitleScreen : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private Button startButton;

    private void Awake()
    {
        var id = startButton.GetComponent<GuaId>()
            ?? startButton.gameObject.AddComponent<GuaId>();
        id.Value = "start";
        startButton.onClick.AddListener(() => Debug.Log("Start Game"));
    }
}`} /><CodeNotes title="Read the uGUI script" items={[
    { code: 'using Gua.Unity;', explanation: 'Imports Unity-facing types such as GuaId.' },
    { code: '[SerializeField] private Button startButton;', explanation: 'Receives the Canvas button through the Inspector.' },
    { code: 'private void Awake()', explanation: 'Prepares identity and listeners before the first collected frame.' },
    { code: 'startButton.GetComponent<GuaId>()', explanation: 'Reuses an existing identity component.' },
    { code: '?? startButton.gameObject.AddComponent<GuaId>()', explanation: 'Adds GuaId only when one is missing.' },
    { code: 'id.Value = "start"', explanation: 'Pins an ID that survives hierarchy refactoring.' },
    { code: 'startButton.onClick.AddListener(...)', explanation: 'Registers the normal uGUI listener used by both users and Gua actions.' },
  ]} /><p>TextMeshPro buttons, input fields, dropdowns, and text are collected from the same Canvas hierarchy. See <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/47515fb/bindings/unity/Samples~/Runtime%20UI%20Fixture/GuaRuntimeUiSample.cs"><code>GuaRuntimeUiSample.cs</code></a> and <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/47515fb/examples/unity-smoke/Assets/GuaUnityFixture.cs"><code>GuaUnityFixture.cs</code></a> for complete examples.</p>
  <h2>5. Add stable identity where needed</h2><p>UI Toolkit prefers <code>viewDataKey</code>, then <code>name</code>. uGUI/TMP prefers <code>GuaId.Value</code>, otherwise it derives an ID from scene path, Transform names, and sibling indices.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Optional identity and screen" lang="csharp" code={`using Gua.Unity;
startButton.gameObject.AddComponent<GuaId>().Value = "start";
screenRoot.AddComponent<GuaScreen>().Value = "title";`} />
  <CodeNotes title="What identity and screen names do" items={[
    { code: 'using Gua.Unity;', explanation: 'Makes GuaId and GuaScreen available.' },
    { code: 'AddComponent<GuaId>().Value = "start"', explanation: 'Exposes the button under stable node ID start. Reuse an existing component in production code.' },
    { code: 'AddComponent<GuaScreen>().Value = "title"', explanation: 'Publishes title as the logical screen name; keep one component per logical screen.' },
  ]} />
  <Callout kind="tip" title="ID contract"><p>Slashes are escaped as <code>~1</code>. IDs over 127 UTF-8 bytes are shortened with a hash suffix. For duplicates in one frame, the first node wins and later nodes are reported in runtime logs.</p></Callout>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Use one GuaScreen"><p>If multiple <code>GuaScreen</code> components exist, the first one found wins, but discovery order is not a public contract. Keep one per logical screen.</p></Callout>
  <h2>6. Publish a World Object Tree</h2><p>To expose a door, enemy, objective, or other non-UI object, add <code>GuaWorldObject</code> to its GameObject. The UPM runtime collects it every frame, so a scene-specific registration loop is unnecessary.</p><CodeBlock title="Publish a door as a game-world object" lang="csharp" code={`using Gua.Core;
using Gua.Unity;
using UnityEngine;

public sealed class ReactorDoor : MonoBehaviour
{
    private GuaWorldObject worldObject;
    private bool locked = true;

    private void Awake()
    {
        worldObject = GetComponent<GuaWorldObject>()
            ?? gameObject.AddComponent<GuaWorldObject>();
        worldObject.Id = "reactor-door";
        worldObject.Kind = "door";
        worldObject.Label = "Reactor door";
        worldObject.Space = GuaWorldSpace.World3D;
        worldObject.VisibleToPlayer = true;
        worldObject.Tags = new[] { "reactor", "mission-critical" };
        PublishState();
    }

    public void SetLocked(bool value)
    {
        locked = value;
        PublishState();
    }

    private void PublishState()
    {
        worldObject.SetState("locked", locked);
        worldObject.SetState("power", "online");
    }
}`} /><p><code>Id</code> is required and unique within the frame. Position comes from <code>transform.position</code>, and the nearest published parent GameObject becomes the parent object. Serialized fields such as ID, kind, visibility, and tags can also be configured on the <code>GuaWorldObject</code> component in the Inspector. Update dynamic state from code through <code>SetState()</code>, passing only strings, numbers, booleans, or null. See <a href="/world-object-tree/">World Object Tree</a> for the conceptual model and query tools.</p>
  <h2>7. Connect external tools</h2><p>The bridge listens on <code>8765</code> by default. Set <code>GUA_BRIDGE_PORT</code> before process startup to override it. Inspector and <code>gui-mcp</code> use the same bridge as other Gua adapters.</p>
  <h2>Export to WebGL (experimental)</h2><p>The released UPM package includes the WebGL native libraries and <code>GuaWebMcp.jslib</code>. Wait until the WebGL build installs the tab-local <code>__guaUnityWebPort</code>, then call the no-argument <code>createUnityWebGlBridge()</code> from <code>gua-webmcp</code> in the same page. The helper resolves that default global name. This path always uses Player projection and does not expose private Debug data.</p><Callout kind="warn" title="Current WebGL limit"><p>The experimental Unity WebGL bridge exposes Semantic UI, opted-in World Object Tree data, and initialized game-input capabilities. It does not currently advertise screenshot support. See the <a href="/webmcp/">browser-native WebMCP guide</a> for registration and security boundaries.</p></Callout>
  <h2>Observation rules</h2><ul><li>Hidden controls remain observable with <code>visible: false</code>; disabled state is reflected separately.</li><li>Bounds use physical screenshot pixels, top-left origin, X rightward, Y downward.</li><li>Actions are applied to Unity controls and completed with a request-correlated result.</li><li>Rendered Player and Play Mode support PNG capture. Batchmode or <code>-nographics</code> reports <code>headless</code>.</li></ul>
  <h2 id="observe">Expose additional UI/World observations</h2><p>Register Observe values explicitly for typed data outside the standard tree. Attach them to a published UI/World owner and unregister when its object is removed. Use <code>CreateObserveOwner</code> on the managed runtime in game code. Player exposure is explicit opt-in. See <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/gua-v1.1.0/protocol/specs/observe-transport-v1.md">engine registration examples</a> and <a href="/docs/gua-runtime/#observe">Value, registration, and external subscriptions</a>.</p>
</DocPage>
